Products designed for single-use fail during subsequent remanufacturing cycles when re-machining, cleaning, and reassembly processes create stresses exceeding design assumptions. Multi-cycle durability requires fundamental design shifts toward robust core materials and construction. Cores designed for multiple cycles create conditions where material value recovery spans 5-10 product lifecycles rather than single cycles.

Core durability design extends material value retention and reduces material throughput compared to single-cycle products. Manufacturing processes optimized for single cycles often become bottlenecks during remanufacturing; robust core design prevents remanufactured product failure during operation.